<BaseLayout title="Research — Skyhook Industries" description="Research heritage behind rotating momentum-exchange tethers, electrodynamic reboost, tether deployment and orbital transportation.">
  <div class="shell">
    <section class="page-hero"><p class="eyebrow">Research</p><h1>Build on what has already been learned.</h1><p>Skyhook Industries is not starting from a blank sheet of paper. Space-tether deployment, electrodynamics and momentum exchange have decades of flight and analytical heritage. The job is to distinguish that heritage from the architecture that has not yet been demonstrated.</p></section>
    <section class="page-section"><div class="grid-3">
      <article class="card research-card"><span class="number">FLIGHT HERITAGE</span><h3>SEDS</h3><p>NASA's Small Expendable Deployer System missions demonstrated deployment of approximately 20 km tethers in orbit and provided important tether-dynamics data.</p><small>Demonstrated: long-tether deployment and dynamics.</small></article>
      <article class="card research-card"><span class="number">FLIGHT HERITAGE</span><h3>TSS-1R</h3><p>The Tethered Satellite System Reflight deployed nearly 20 km of conducting tether and measured strong electrodynamic interaction with the ionospheric plasma and geomagnetic field.</p><small>Demonstrated: current collection and electrodynamic effects.</small></article>
      <article class="card research-card"><span class="number">FLIGHT HERITAGE</span><h3>YES2</h3><p>The Young Engineers' Satellite 2 deployed a roughly 32 km tether and used tether dynamics to release a capsule onto a re-entry trajectory.</p><small>Demonstrated: tether-assisted momentum exchange.</small></article>
    </div></section>
    <section class="page-section prose"><p class="eyebrow">Transportation studies</p><h2>HASTOL and momentum-exchange architectures</h2><p>NASA Institute for Advanced Concepts work and Boeing-led HASTOL studies examined hypersonic aircraft rendezvous with rotating orbital tethers, capture dynamics, structural sizing and electrodynamic reboost. These studies are especially relevant because they treated the tether as transportation infrastructure rather than as a small science experiment.</p><p>They should not be presented as proof that Skyhook Industries' present geometry is already solved. Their value is that they expose the coupled engineering problem in enough detail to build from.</p></section>
    <section class="page-section prose"><p class="eyebrow">Research standard</p><h2>Four kinds of claims.</h2><ul class="list-clean"><li><b>Flight heritage</b><span>Measured or demonstrated in space.</span></li><li><b>Prior research</b><span>Modeled or analyzed in credible aerospace literature.</span></li><li><b>Skyhook design direction</b><span>An architectural choice we are presently exploring.</span></li><li><b>Open target</b><span>A value or feature that still depends on modeling, testing or material performance.</span></li></ul></section>
    <section class="page-section prose"><p class="eyebrow">Primary starting points</p><h2>Selected literature</h2><ul><li>NASA / Boeing — Hypersonic Airplane Space Tether Orbital Launch (HASTOL), Phase I and Phase II.</li><li>Robert P. Hoyt and Chauncey Uphoff — Cislunar Tether Transport System.</li><li>NASA — Tethers in Space Handbook and electrodynamic tether flight literature.</li><li>SEDS, TSS-1R and YES2 mission publications and post-flight analyses.</li></ul><p>The bibliography will expand alongside the reference architecture, with primary sources preferred over summaries.</p></section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
